---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro'

const post = {
 title: 'Bulk Ordering vs Company Store: The Real Math for Contractors',
 description:
 'Compare bulk apparel ordering with a proposed company-store program. Review roster, storage, approvals, product, fulfillment, and quoted costs before choosing.',
 category: 'Operations',
 readTime: '8 min read',
 date: 'Apr 1, 2026',
 isoDate: '2026-04-01',
 image: '/images/generated/product-stack.jpg',
 slug: 'company-store-vs-bulk-ordering',
}

const faqs = [
 { q: 'What costs should you review with bulk apparel?', a: 'Review product and decoration cost, size accuracy, storage, internal handling, replacement, obsolete artwork, and unused garments. The effect varies by company, so use your own order history.' },
 { q: 'How does a branded company store work?', a: 'A proposed store could present approved products and support size or order submission if the selected platform and approved configuration do. No platform has been selected or launched. Payment, delivery, fulfillment, and all capabilities require separate written approval.' },
 { q: 'At what crew size does a company store beat bulk ordering?', a: 'There is no universal crew-size threshold. Compare quoted program costs with your roster, order frequency, turnover, approval process, storage, delivery needs, and staff time.' },
 { q: 'Why can a company store help with new-hire onboarding?', a: 'A proposed store could support new-hire size submission against approved products if the selected platform and configuration do. No platform has been selected or launched. Implementation, delivery, timing, and capabilities require separate written approval.' },
 { q: 'When should you still order in bulk instead of using a company store?', a: 'Bulk may suit a defined event, a stable roster, or one approved product. A company store may suit recurring, role-based ordering. Compare both against the same products, quantities, fees, and delivery terms.' },
 { q: 'How do you measure the true cost of work apparel?', a: 'Track invoice cost, internal handling, storage, unused garments, replacements, delivery, program fees, and staff time. Use your own records and a current written quote for each option.' },
]

const schema = {
 '@context': 'https://schema.org',
 '@graph': [
 {
 '@type': 'Article',
 headline: post.title,
 description: post.description,
 image: `https://bighornthreads.com${post.image}`,
 datePublished: post.isoDate,
 dateModified: '2026-08-21',
 author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Bighorn Threads', url: 'https://bighornthreads.com/' },
 publisher: {
 '@type': 'Organization',
 name: 'Bighorn Threads',
 logo: {
 '@type': 'ImageObject',
 url: 'https://bighornthreads.com/images/logos/bighorn-logo-color.png',
 },
 },
 mainEntityOfPage: `https://bighornthreads.com/blog/${post.slug}/`,
 },
 {
 '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
 itemListElement: [
 { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://bighornthreads.com/' },
 { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Blog', item: 'https://bighornthreads.com/blog/' },
 {
 '@type': 'ListItem',
 position: 3,
 name: post.title,
 item: `https://bighornthreads.com/blog/${post.slug}/`,
 },
 ],
 },
 {
 '@type': 'FAQPage',
 mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
 '@type': 'Question',
 name: f.q,
 acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
 })),
 },
 {
 '@type': 'WebPage',
 url: `https://bighornthreads.com/blog/${post.slug}/`,
 speakable: { '@type': 'SpeakableSpecification', cssSelector: ['h1', '.speakable-answer'] },
 },
 ],
}
---

<BaseLayout
 title={post.title}
 description={post.description}
 canonical={`https://bighornthreads.com/blog/${post.slug}/`}
 ogImage={post.image}
 type="article"
 publishedDate={post.isoDate}
>
 <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />

 <article class="bg-concrete">
 <header class="border-b border-hairline bg-paper">

 <div class="relative mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 pt-20 pb-12 sm:px-6 sm:pt-24 lg:px-8">
 <nav class="mb-6 font-mono text-xs text-ash" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
 <a href="/" class="hover:text-gold-700">Home</a>
 <span class="px-2">/</span>
 <a href="/blog/" class="hover:text-gold-700">Blog</a>
 </nav>

 <span class="mb-3 inline-block text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gold-600">
 {post.category}
 </span>
 <h1 class="mb-6 font-heading text-3xl font-bold leading-tight tracking-tight sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl">
 {post.title}
 </h1>
 <p class="mb-8 text-xl leading-relaxed text-ash">
 Bulk and company-store programs solve different ordering problems. Compare both with your real roster, process, and written costs.
 </p>

 <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-4 gap-y-2 border-t border-hairline pt-6 font-mono text-sm text-ash">
 <span class="text-gold-600">Bighorn Threads Team</span>
 <span aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
 <span>{post.readTime}</span>
 <span aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
 <time datetime={post.isoDate}>{post.date}</time>
 </div>
 </div>
 </header>

 <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 pt-10 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
 <img
 src={post.image}
 alt="Stack of folded branded construction apparel including polos, tees, and hats ready for distribution"
 width="1200"
 height="675"
 loading="eager"
 class="w-full border border-hairline object-cover"
 />
 </div>

 <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
 <!-- TOC -->
 <aside class="mb-12 border border-hairline bg-panel p-6 lg:sticky lg:top-24">
 <h2 class="mb-4 font-mono text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gold-600">In this article</h2>
 <ol class="space-y-2 font-mono text-sm text-charcoal">
 <li><a href="#hidden-costs" class="hover:text-gold-700">1. The hidden costs of bulk ordering</a></li>
 <li><a href="#how-stores-work" class="hover:text-gold-700">2. How company stores actually work</a></li>
 <li><a href="#thresholds" class="hover:text-gold-700">3. Crew size thresholds</a></li>
 <li><a href="#onboarding" class="hover:text-gold-700">4. New-hire onboarding</a></li>
 <li><a href="#real-numbers" class="hover:text-gold-700">5. Cost comparison with real numbers</a></li>
 <li><a href="#still-bulk" class="hover:text-gold-700">6. When you should still bulk order</a></li>
 </ol>
 </aside>

 <p class="speakable-answer mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Bulk ordering can work well for a defined roster and product list. A company-store program may help with recurring, role-based orders. The right choice depends on your operations and quote.
 </p>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 The question isn't "bulk or company store." The question is "what does my actual cost per wearable piece look like once I account for everything that goes wrong." That's where the math gets interesting.
 </p>

 <h2 id="hidden-costs" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">1. The Hidden Costs of Bulk Ordering</h2>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Start with the invoice, then review four operational costs that may affect your program:
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold">Wrong sizes</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Estimated size curves can miss your actual roster. Collect sizes when possible and decide how your team will handle exchanges and unused garments.
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold">Shelf storage</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Bulk apparel requires storage, counts, distribution, and reorder ownership. Estimate the space and staff time from your own process.
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold">Turnover & dead inventory</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Hiring and turnover can change the size mix. Review your replacement policy and unused-garment history before setting a buffer.
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold">Logo changes & discontinued colors</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Rebrands and discontinued products can make stored garments harder to use. Keep the buffer tied to a documented need.
 </p>

 <div class="my-10 border-l-4 border-gold-500 bg-gold-500/5 p-6">
 <p class="mb-2 font-mono text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gold-600">Key takeaway</p>
 <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Include unused garments, storage, and internal handling when you compare options.
 </p>
 </div>

 <h2 id="how-stores-work" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">2. How Company Stores Actually Work</h2>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 A proposed <a href="/company-stores/" class="text-gold-600 underline hover:text-gold-700">branded company store</a> could present approved products and support size or order submission if the selected platform and approved configuration do. No platform has been selected or launched. Delivery, fulfillment, implementation, and capabilities require separate written approval. See the <a href="/company-stores/contractors-las-vegas/" class="text-gold-600 underline hover:text-gold-700">contractor store planning guide</a>.
 </p>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 There are three main ways to fund the store:
 </p>

 <ul class="mb-6 list-disc space-y-3 pl-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li><strong class="text-charcoal">Company-paid.</strong> Company pays for everything, usually with an annual allowance per employee (depending on role).</li>
 <li><strong class="text-charcoal">Employee-paid.</strong> Employees buy their own gear at cost or slight markup. Works well for discretionary items like hoodies and hats.</li>
 <li><strong class="text-charcoal">Hybrid.</strong> Core required items (safety vests, hi-vis tees, PM polos) are company-paid. Everything else is employee-paid.</li>
 </ul>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Hybrid funding is one option to evaluate. The buyer must approve required items, employee-paid items, payment handling, and program terms before implementation.
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold">Fulfillment model</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Inventory ownership, decoration schedule, packaging, delivery, and internal handling depend on the selected program. Confirm each responsibility in writing before launch.
 </p>

 <h2 id="thresholds" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">3. Crew Size Thresholds — When Each Makes Sense</h2>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 There is no universal crew-size threshold. Use these operational questions instead:
 </p>

 <ul class="mb-6 list-disc space-y-3 pl-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li><strong class="text-charcoal">Roster.</strong> Are sizes and roles stable enough for a planned bulk order?</li>
 <li><strong class="text-charcoal">Order frequency.</strong> Do you place recurring orders throughout the year?</li>
 <li><strong class="text-charcoal">Approvals.</strong> Who controls products, artwork, allowances, and purchasing?</li>
 <li><strong class="text-charcoal">Fulfillment.</strong> Who stores, packs, delivers, and resolves exceptions?</li>
 </ul>

 <h2 id="onboarding" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">4. New-Hire Onboarding — The Killer Use Case</h2>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 New-hire ordering is one use case to evaluate during program design.
 </p>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 With bulk ordering, new-hire onboarding means somebody in the office walks to the closet, guesses at sizes, and hands the new hire a stack of gear on day one. Half the time the sizes are wrong and the office manager is back in the closet by lunch.
 </p>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 A proposed company store could support new-hire size submission if the selected platform and approved configuration do. No platform has been selected or launched. Implementation, delivery, product availability, timing, and capabilities require separate written approval.
 </p>


 <h2 id="real-numbers" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">5. Cost Comparison With Real Numbers</h2>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Every order and program requires a current quote. Compare the same products, decoration, quantities, fees, delivery, storage, and staff time. A company store may reduce some internal handling, but the written setup defines the result.
 </p>

 <div class="my-10 border border-hairline bg-panel p-6">
 <p class="mb-2 font-mono text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gold-600">Pro tip</p>
 <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Run the numbers on your last bulk order. Count the pieces still sitting on the shelf. Divide the invoice total by the number that actually got worn. Most contractors are surprised by what they find.
 </p>
 </div>

 <h2 id="still-bulk" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">6. When You Should Still Bulk Order</h2>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Company stores aren't always the answer. You're better off with a straight bulk order when:
 </p>

 <ul class="mb-6 list-disc space-y-3 pl-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li><strong class="text-charcoal">One-off events.</strong> Groundbreakings, topping-outs, company BBQs, and charity walks. Order 50 tees, hand them out, done.</li>
 <li><strong class="text-charcoal">Seasonal promotional runs.</strong> Giveaway gear for trade shows or community events where the logo is the point and nobody cares about fit.</li>
 <li><strong class="text-charcoal">Small, stable crews.</strong> Under 15 people, low turnover, same sizes year after year. A store is more overhead than it's worth.</li>
 <li><strong class="text-charcoal">Specialty items.</strong> A premium jacket run for leadership or a limited-edition company anniversary piece.</li>
 </ul>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Some companies use both models. The company store can cover recurring approved products, while bulk orders handle defined events or campaigns. Confirm each program separately.
 </p>

 <!-- Mid-article CTA -->
 <div class="my-12 border border-hairline bg-gold-500/10 p-8 text-center">
 <h3 class="mb-3 font-heading text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Curious if a company store makes sense for your crew?</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-charcoal">
 Share your current process and goals. We'll outline a proposal with the applicable products, fees, and responsibilities.
 </p>
 <a
 href="/company-stores/"
 class="inline-flex min-h-[44px] items-center justify-center bg-gold-500 px-8 py-4 text-lg font-bold text-charcoal transition-all hover:bg-gold-600"
 >
 Explore Company Stores
 </a>
 </div>

 <h2 class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">The Short Version</h2>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Compare bulk and company-store options with your own roster, order history, internal handling, and written costs. No crew-size threshold guarantees one model will cost less.
 </p>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 If you're growing, hiring, or just tired of managing an apparel closet, stop measuring by invoice and start measuring by worn-piece cost. The answer changes.
 </p>

